    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That's a good start. Can you go a bit deeper into the pros and cons of each? Specifically, I want to explain why horizontal scaling is usually better for long-term growth and reliability in a cloud environment like AWS. Why wouldn't we just keep upgrading the same server indefinitely?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Vertical scaling is simpler to implement initially but has strict limits on how powerful one machine can become, creating a single point of failure. Conversely, horizontal scaling offers near-limitless scalability simply by adding more nodes, which is ideal for cloud elasticity. Furthermore, if one server in a horizontally scaled cluster fails, others can immediately take over, significantly boosting overall system reliability. Therefore, for resilient and massive cloud growth, spreading the load is the superior long-term strategy.",
      "source": "target",
